{
 "idea": {
  "id": "us_lead",
  "family": "cross-market",
  "title": "Asian and Australian markets from their open to their close, in the direction New York moved overnight",
  "why": "Tokyo, Hong Kong, Seoul, Taipei and Sydney open a few hours after New York closes. The opening gap prices most of the US move, but local investors who trade through the day (and foreign money that arrives during it) may keep adjusting, so the rest of the session could continue the US move. This is a question about how fast local prices absorb overnight US news. Entry is at the local open, after the US close is known, and exit at the local close. In Korea and Taiwan the short days stand for an index future (no ETF shorting). Run per market as us_lead@hk and so on; every market gets the same variants.",
  "source": "Lead-lag between US and Asian markets: Eun and Shim 1989; Becker, Finnerty and Gupta 1990",
